The spelling is laissez-faire, and no—not automatically.

If by “sexual marketplace” you mean adults freely choosing partners without authorities assigning relationships, then the laissez-faire principle already applies in its strongest legitimate form: free association and mutual consent.

But human intimacy is not an ordinary commodity market. People are subjects, not products; consent can be withdrawn; access cannot be purchased as an entitlement; and unequal power, deception, coercion, age, dependency, disease risk, pregnancy, and harm to families create externalities.

Even economic markets are never truly ruleless. They require property definitions, contract enforcement, fraud prohibitions, and protection against force. The corresponding intimate framework is:

\text{freedom of association}
+\text{informed consent}
+\text{honesty}
+\text{reciprocity}
+\text{responsibility for consequences}

Drago’s counterpunch: “sexual marketplace” is useful for studying competition, scarcity, signaling, and preference—but dangerous when the metaphor starts treating people as inventory or rejection as theft.

So yes to decentralized choice. No to entitlement, coercion, or pretending private desire has no relational consequences. Nobody owes anyone access; nobody owns another person’s choices.